-- title:
--   Weighted blow-up chart C[J/varpiₜ] and its exceptional valuation ring
-- statement:
--   Arithmetic data. Let $q\ge 5$ and $\ell\ge 3$ be primes with $\ell\neq q$, and let $M'$ be a nonzero natural number with $q\nmid M'$ and $\ell\nmid M'$. Let $L$ be a field of characteristic zero and $\xi\in L$ a primitive $(q\ell)$-th root of unity, and assume (`hι`) that there is a ring homomorphism $\iota:L\to\mathbb{C}$ with $\iota\xi=\exp(2\pi i/(q\ell))$. Let $K$ be an intermediate field of $L\subseteq L(\!(t)\!)$ (Laurent series over $L$) and assume (`hK`) that $K=\,$[`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.xHFunctionField ((q*ℓ)^2*M') (ModularCurve.FullLevel.levelH (q*ℓ) M'))`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), i.e. $K$ is the $L$-subfield of $L(\!(t)\!)$ generated by the coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion function field of the modular curve $X_H$ of level $(q\ell)^2M'$, where $H=\,$`levelH (q*ℓ) M'` is the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb{Z}/(q\ell)^2M')^\times\to(\mathbb{Z}/q\ell)^\times$, that is, the units congruent to $1$ modulo $q\ell$.
--
--   Let $A$ be a Henselian discrete valuation ring with algebraically closed residue field, equipped with an $L$-algebra structure making $L$ its fraction field, with $q\in\mathfrak{m}_A$ (`hAq`) and $\xi$ in the image of $A$ (`hξA`); $K$ is an $A$-algebra compatibly with $L$. Let $j\in K$ be nonzero with Laurent expansion $\,$[`ModularCurve.coeffEmb L ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81), the $q$-expansion of the modular function $j$ pushed from $\mathbb{Q}$ to $L$ (`hj`). Let $\varpi$ generate $\mathfrak{m}_A$ (`hϖ`), and let $\varpi_t\in A$ satisfy $\varpi_t^{\,q^2-1}=q\,u$ for some unit $u$ (`hϖt`).
--
--   Write $C=\,$`chartAlgFin A ↥K j`, the $A$-subalgebra of elements of $K$ integral over $A[j]$, and let $\mathfrak{X}=\,$[`AlgebraicCurve.TwoChartIntegralModel A ↥K j`](def/AlgebraicCurve_TwoChartIntegralModel.html#L236) be the two-chart integral model, the pushout of the two maps $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the middle chart algebra into $\operatorname{Spec} C$ and into $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the corresponding algebra for $j^{-1}$, together with its structure morphism `toBase` to $\operatorname{Spec} A$. Let $y\subset C$ be a maximal ideal (`hy`) containing $\varpi$ (`hϖy`) which is supersingular in the sense (`hss`) that for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring homomorphism $\varphi:C\to\Omega$ with kernel $y$, the element $\varphi(j)$ lies in [`ModularCurve.ssJSet q Ω`](def/ModularCurve_SupersingularModuli.html#L7), the set of $\bar j\in\Omega$ such that every elliptic curve over $\Omega$ with $j$-invariant $\bar j$ has no nonzero $\Omega$-rational point killed by $q$.
--
--   The rigidity hypothesis `hArig`. It is assumed that at every point $z$ of $\mathfrak{X}$ at which the germ $\varpi_z$ of (the pullback of) $\varpi$ lies in the maximal ideal of the stalk, and for every point $y'$ of $\operatorname{Spec} C$ mapping to $z$ under `ιFin` and satisfying the same supersingularity condition as above for $y'$, there exist a complete discrete valuation ring $W$, a ring homomorphism $\sigma:A\to W$ with $\mathfrak{m}_W=(\sigma\varpi)$, power series $f,u,v\in W[[X_0,X_1]]$ with $u,v$ units and $f\equiv X_0X_1^{\,q}-X_0^{\,q}X_1$ modulo $(X_0,X_1)^{q+2}$ (the form [`DrinfeldCurve.LocalChart.drinfeldForm q W`](def/DrinfeldCurve_LocalChart.html#L18)), and a ring isomorphism
--   $$e:\ \widehat{\mathcal{O}}_{\mathfrak{X},z}\ \xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }\ S:=W[[X_0,X_1]]\big/\big(\sigma(\varpi_t^{\,q+1})v-fu\big),$$
--   the completion being the adic completion of the stalk at its maximal ideal, subject to seven clauses (summarised here), stated in terms of the canonical maps $\mathrm{toC}$ from the stalk to its completion, $\mathrm{mkS}$ from $W[[X_0,X_1]]$ to $S$, and the germ map $\mathrm{germY}:C\to\mathcal{O}_{\mathfrak{X},z}$: (i) $e$ carries the germ of $a\in A$ to the class of the constant $\sigma a$; (ii) for $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$, every $L$-automorphism $\tau$ of $K$ satisfying [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.IsLevelAutAt L (q*ℓ) ξ (q*ℓ) ((q*ℓ)^2*M') (levelH (q*ℓ) M') γ⁻¹ K τ`](def/ModularCurve_FullLevelLevelAutAt.html#L29) preserves $C$; (iii) for $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')\cap\Gamma(\ell)$ such a $\tau$ satisfies $\tau(a)-a\in y'$ for all $a\in C$; (iv) for $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$ and such a $\tau$ preserving $C$ and fixing $C$ modulo $y'$, there are an automorphism $\theta$ of $S$ compatible with $\tau$ through $e\circ \mathrm{toC}\circ\mathrm{germY}$, a scalar $c\in W$ and a matrix $M\in M_2(W)$ such that $\theta$ fixes constants, $\theta(X_k)\equiv\sum_i M_{ik}X_i$ modulo the square of $(X_0,X_1)$, $c^{q+1}\equiv 1$ and $M_{ik}\equiv c\,\gamma_{ik}$ modulo $\mathfrak{m}_W$, with $c\equiv 1$ when $\gamma\in\Gamma(\ell)$ and $c\not\equiv 1$ when $\gamma\in\Gamma(q)$ and $\tau\neq\mathrm{id}$; (v) two primes of $S$ lying over $\sigma\varpi$, neither containing both $X_0$ and $X_1$, and containing elements $a_iX_0+b_iX_1+h_i$ with $h_i\in(X_0,X_1)^2$, have distinct contractions to the stalk whenever $q\nmid a_1b_2-a_2b_1$; (vi) for a prime $P$ of $S$ of the same kind containing $X_0+h$ with $h\in(X_0,X_1)^2$, an element $a\in C$ has its germ in the contraction of $P$ precisely when every Laurent coefficient of $a$ lies in the image of $\mathfrak{m}_A$; (vii) the Laurent series [`ModularCurve.jqNModC L (q*ℓ)`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L18) (the $q$-expansion of $j$ with exponents scaled by $q\ell$) lies in $K$ and in $C$, and for a suitable $a_0\in A$ its difference with $a_0$ lies in $y'$ and is carried by $e\circ\mathrm{toC}\circ\mathrm{germY}$ to the class of some $h\in(X_0,X_1)^{e_0}$, $e_0\ge 1$, whose degree-$e_0$ form $\sum_{i\le e_0}\mathrm{coeff}_{(i,e_0-i)}(h)\,a^ib^{e_0-i}$ is a unit for all $a,b\in W$ with $a$ or $b$ a unit and $a^qb-ab^q\in\mathfrak{m}_W$. Here `IsLevelAutAt` says that for every weight $k$, every pair $f,g$ of modular forms for $\Gamma_H$ of level $(q\ell)^2M'$ with integral $q$-expansions $p_f,p_g$, $p_g$ having nonzero Laurent series, every $x\in K$ whose Laurent expansion is the image of $p_f/p_g$ under coefficient extension from $\mathbb{Q}$, and every $\iota:L\to\mathbb{C}$ with $\iota\xi=\exp(2\pi i/(q\ell))$, the $\iota$-image of the Laurent expansion of $\tau x$, multiplied by the $q$-expansion of $g\mid_k\mathrm{conjElemN}\,(q\ell)\,\gamma^{-1}$, equals the $q$-expansion of $f\mid_k\mathrm{conjElemN}\,(q\ell)\,\gamma^{-1}$, with $\mathrm{conjElemN}\,m\,\delta=\begin{pmatrix}\delta_{00}&\delta_{01}/m\\ m\delta_{10}&\delta_{11}\end{pmatrix}$.
--
--   The fixed witness. In addition there are fixed data: a point $z$ of $\mathfrak{X}$, the germ $\varpi_z$ of $\varpi$ at $z$ lying in the maximal ideal of the stalk, a point $y'$ of $\operatorname{Spec} C$ with `ιFin`-image $z$, satisfying the supersingularity condition (`hss'`), and $y'$ is the given $y$ (`hy'y`); and one specific witness $(W_1,\sigma_1,f_1,u_1,v_1,e_1)$ of the above shape at $z$, with $W_1$ a complete discrete valuation ring, $\mathfrak{m}_{W_1}=(\sigma_1\varpi)$, $u_1,v_1$ units, $f_1\equiv X_0X_1^{\,q}-X_0^{\,q}X_1$ modulo $(X_0,X_1)^{q+2}$, and $e_1$ an isomorphism of the completed stalk with $S_1=W_1[[X_0,X_1]]/(\sigma_1(\varpi_t^{\,q+1})v_1-f_1u_1)$, for which the hypothesis `hW₁` asserts the same seven clauses (i)–(vii), verbatim for this witness.
--
--   The centre. Finally, $J$ is an ideal of $C$ pinned by `hJ`: $J$ is the infimum of the set of ideals $J'$ of $C$ for which there are $\gamma\in\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$ and an $L$-automorphism $\tau$ of $K$ with `IsLevelAutAt … γ⁻¹ K τ` preserving $C$, such that $J'$ is the pullback along the restriction of $\tau$ to $C$ of the contraction along $e_1\circ\mathrm{toC}\circ\mathrm{germY}$ of the ideal of $S_1$ generated by the classes of $\sigma_1\varpi_t$, $X_0$ and $X_1$.
--
--   Conclusion. Under these hypotheses there exist an $A$-subalgebra $B$ of $K$, a valuation subring $W$ of $K$, and a proof that $B\subseteq W$, such that:
--
--   (1) $B$ is, as an $A$-algebra, the $C$-subalgebra of $K$ generated by $\{x\in K:\exists\, i\in J,\ x\,\varpi_t=i\}$, i.e. $B=C[J/\varpi_t]$;
--
--   (2) $C\le B$;
--
--   (3) every $f\in K$ is a quotient of elements of $B$: there are $g,h\in B$ with $h\neq 0$ and $f\,h=g$;
--
--   (4) $B$ is formally smooth over $A$;
--
--   (5) $B$ is of finite presentation over $A$;
--
--   (6) the special fibre $B/(\varpi)$ has Krull dimension at most $1$;
--
--   (7) for $x\in L$, the image of $x$ in $K$ lies in $W$ if and only if $x$ comes from $A$, so $W\cap L=A$;
--
--   (8) the maximal ideal of $W$ is generated by the image of $\varpi$;
--
--   (9) $W$ is a discrete valuation ring;
--
--   (10) for $b\in C$, $b$ lies in $y$ if and only if $b$ lies in $W$ and its image there lies in the maximal ideal of $W$, so the centre of $W$ on $C$ is $y$;
--
--   (11) for $f\in K$, $f$ lies in $W$ if and only if there are $g,h\in B$ with $h\notin\mathfrak{m}_W$ and $f\,h=g$, so $W$ is the localisation of $B$ at the prime $\mathfrak{m}_W\cap B$.
--
--   This is the algebraisation step in the construction of a supersingular chart for the integral model: the weighted blow-up $B=C[J/\varpi_t]$ of the $j$-finite chart algebra along the $\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$-invariant centre $J$ is produced together with the exceptional discrete valuation ring $W$, and the formal Drinfeld presentation of the completed stalk is transferred into the statements that $B$ is formally smooth and of finite presentation over $A$ with one-dimensional special fibre, has $K$ as fraction field, and is dominated by $W$ with centre $y$ on the chart algebra. It is used by the assembly producing a supersingular affine chart with its ends, poles and inertia data, the other parts of which take the pair $(B,W)$ and its defining equation as hypotheses.
